SELECT(2)                      P32W Unix                       SELECT(2)

NAME
       select - wait for any of several files

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/select.h>

       int select(int nfds, fd_set *rd, fd_set *wr, fd_set *ex,
                  struct timeval *timeout);
       FD_ZERO(&set); FD_SET(fd, &set); FD_CLR(fd, &set);
       FD_ISSET(fd, &set)

DESCRIPTION
       select waits until one of the descriptors in rd has
       something to read, or one in wr has room to write, or the
       timeout passes, and leaves in each set only the descriptors
       that are ready. A null set is not looked at. A null timeout
       waits for ever; a zero one only looks.

       Ready to read: a terminal with a line typed (a character, in
       CBREAK or RAW mode); a pipe with data, or no writer left; a
       socket with data, a connection waiting (listening), the end
       of the stream, or an error. Ready to write: a pipe with
       room; a stream socket that is connected with room to send
       (or that failed); a datagram socket always. Files and other
       devices are always ready. ex is never filled in.

       nfds is ignored: an fd_set is one word, and a process has at
       most 20 descriptors.

RETURN VALUE
       The number of descriptors ready (0 if the timeout passed); -1
       on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EBADF   A set names a descriptor that is not open.
       EINTR   A signal came.
       EINVAL  A negative timeout.

SEE ALSO
       socket(2), fcntl(2), read(2)

BUGS
       The timeout counts clock ticks (1/50 s). Nothing is ever
       exceptional.
